\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The three methods on the same $119/120$ point grid, CO$_2$ case. Left, the temperatures: indistinguishable at this scale. Right, the difference with the discrete ordinates, taken as reference because it is the smooth and grid converged one. The Monte Carlo oscillates about zero without any trend --- statistical noise, which decreases like $N^{-1/2}$ --- at most $0.27\,^\circ $C, $0.09\,^\circ $C r.m.s.; ISIF departs smoothly and systematically, $-0.6\,^\circ $C at the ground, $+0.2\,^\circ $C at the tropopause, at most $0.63\,^\circ $C and $0.24\,^\circ $C r.m.s.}}{6}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:three}{{3}{6}{The three methods on the same $119/120$ point grid, CO$_2$ case. Left, the temperatures: indistinguishable at this scale. Right, the difference with the discrete ordinates, taken as reference because it is the smooth and grid converged one. The Monte Carlo oscillates about zero without any trend --- statistical noise, which decreases like $N^{-1/2}$ --- at most $0.27\,^\circ $C, $0.09\,^\circ $C r.m.s.; ISIF departs smoothly and systematically, $-0.6\,^\circ $C at the ground, $+0.2\,^\circ $C at the tropopause, at most $0.63\,^\circ $C and $0.24\,^\circ $C r.m.s}{figure.3}{}}
